--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The interpreter in this directory is written for Braam and carries the MIT
terms above, which are this repository's. So do the shims under test/shim/,
which are not copies of anyone's code.

Two test suites here are somebody else's, and each carries its own licence.

The files under test/cases/ are MicroPython's test suite, copied byte for byte
from https://github.com/micropython/micropython under the MIT licence below.
test/manifest.txt records, for each one, the upstream path and the commit it
was taken from.

The files under test/cpython/ are CPython's own test suite, copied byte for
byte from https://github.com/python/cpython under the PSF licence below.
test/cpython.txt records, for each one, the upstream path and the commit it was
taken from.

The files under lib/ are modules of CPython's own standard library, copied byte
for byte from the same place and under the same PSF licence.
lib/manifest.txt records, for each one, the upstream path, the commit it was
taken from, and the native module it stands on.

The data files CPython's tests read, under test/cpython/test/, are CPython's
too, under the same licence; test/cpython/data.txt records where each came
from.

sre.cpp is the matching engine of CPython's Modules/_sre/ (sre_lib.h and the
engine half of sre.c), rewritten here, and sremod.cpp follows the rest of
sre.c. Secret Labs wrote that engine, and it may be redistributed under CNRI's
Python 1.6 licence below; CPython distributes it under the PSF licence below.

csvmod.cpp is CPython's Modules/_csv.c rewritten here, and iobase.cpp,
iofile.cpp, iobuf.cpp, iotext.cpp and iomem.cpp follow Modules/_io/ in their
checks, their order and their messages, with textio.c's tell() cookie; they
are under the PSF licence below.

The files under src/expat/ are libexpat 2.8.4, rewritten here in C++ from
CPython's copy of it in Modules/expat/, under the MIT licence they carry at
the head of each file. siphash.h is William Ahern's, under CC0, as its own
header says. pyexpatmod.cpp is written here and answers CPython's
Modules/pyexpat.c, so it is under the PSF licence below.

ucddb.cpp and ucddb.h are generated by tools/mkucd.py from the Unicode
Character Database, version 16.0.0, and from its version 3.2.0 as the host
CPython's unicodedata.ucd_3_2_0 answers it, under the Unicode licence below.

